# 12. The Terms actually give us a very good discovery framework

I would build the request around these contractual statements:

| BitMEX's December 2019 Terms                                          | What discovery should determine                                                                  |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **"responsibility to maintain an orderly market"** (§1.12)            | What did BitMEX consider an "orderly market" during March 13? What were the suspension criteria? |
| May halt trading for **market disruption** (§1.12)                    | Was suspension considered? Who decided not to halt?                                              |
| **"reasonable efforts"** to ensure availability (§7.3)                | What availability controls existed?                                                              |
| **"commercially reasonable efforts"** to avoid downtime (§7.3)        | What capacity, DDoS, redundancy and contingency measures existed?                                |
| Terms expressly anticipate **system overloads** (§7.3)                | What overload testing/protection existed?                                                        |
| Customers must report material connectivity problems (§7.5)           | How many complaints/tickets were received during March 13?                                       |
| Trading Platform includes **APIs** (§Definitions)                     | Were API/order/cancel/close pathways actually functional?                                        |
| API instructions include **closing positions** (§1.7)                 | Could customers close/reduce positions?                                                          |
| Affiliated trading arm supposedly gets **same access** (§6.3)         | Did the trading arm have better access during the outage?                                        |
| BitMEX can **halt trading** (§1.12)                                   | Was that functionality technically available in March?                                           |
| Force majeure limited to matters outside **reasonable control** (§14) | What was known after the February attack and what remediation occurred?                          |

That table belongs in your report.

## And it leads to one particularly important discovery request

I would specifically request:

> **All documents, communications, policies, procedures, runbooks, technical specifications, source-code change records, engineering tickets, incident reports and decision records in effect from February 1 through May 31, 2020 concerning (i) platform availability; (ii) maintenance of an orderly market; (iii) market suspension; (iv) liquidation suspension; (v) Cancel-Only mode; (vi) DDoS response; (vii) system overload; (viii) customer order cancellation and position-closing functionality during overload; and (ix) criteria governing the suspension and resumption of trading.**

Then separately request:

> **All documents showing when the May 19, 2020 Market Suspended / Cancel-Only and no-liquidation procedures were designed, developed, tested, approved or implemented, including documents identifying whether those procedures resulted from lessons learned during the March 12–13, 2020 incident.**

That second request could be particularly important.

If discovery shows **Cancel-Only/no-liquidation functionality already existed in March but wasn't activated**, that's one story.

If it didn't exist until after March, but internal records show BitMEX immediately developed it because March exposed the danger, that's another story.

If BitMEX considered suspension in March and affirmatively rejected it despite knowing customers could not access their positions, that's another.

**We don't yet know which one is true—and that's exactly why these Terms give you a credible basis for seeking the discovery.**

I would make **§1.12 ("responsibility to maintain an orderly market") + §7.3 ("reasonable efforts"/"commercially reasonable efforts") + BitMEX's March admission that users could barely reach the engine + the May 19 cancel-only/no-liquidation response** the four pillars of the argument.

